In a bustling online retail company called "TechGadgets Inc.", user Mia Thompson is managing the inventory for the latest smartphone models. The company utilizes a GraphQL API to streamline their inventory tracking system, which is hosted on a secure Linux server. 

Mia, a tech-savvy inventory manager, needs to check the stock levels of the newly launched "SmartX Pro" smartphone, which has a SKU of 12345. To do this, she needs to execute a GraphQL query via an SSH client connected to the server. She opens her SSH terminal and types the command to connect to the server using her credentials. 

Once logged in, Mia formulates a GraphQL query to fetch the current stock levels from the "Inventory" table. The query looks like this:

```
query {
  inventory(sku: "12345") {
    productName
    stockLevel
  }
}
```

After executing the query, she receives a response indicating that there are currently 50 units of the "SmartX Pro" available in the inventory. Satisfied with the information, Mia decides to place an order for an additional 100 units to ensure they don't run out of stock during the holiday season.

